---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="The same hand pressing the same button five times over, each impression printed further out of register than the last, in flat gold and black on cream — a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="thesis">
    The answer is usually yes. That is exactly what makes it dangerous:
    retrying looks like diligence, gets rewarded almost every time, and is
    never free. Every fix in this course buys something and spends something
    else, and the skill being taught is naming the price before you pay it.
  </p>

  <h2>Twelve weeks, twelve situations</h2>
  <p>
    Not twelve topics. Every week takes the same question into a new
    situation, and the situation is always concrete enough to reproduce: a
    read that times out once, a payment that is charged twice, ten thousand
    clients that all come back on the same second, two people who resend the
    same payout at 3am with no code between them at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every week ends the same way, too — with the fix, and with what the fix
    costs. Backoff buys a struggling dependency room and spends your latency
    to do it. A circuit breaker gives up availability you might have had. A
    deadline throws away work that was about to succeed. By week 12 you should
    be unable to propose a retry policy without naming its price, which is the
    entire skill this course is trying to teach.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year students who have written code that talks to something over a
    network and have already been surprised by it. You need to be comfortable
    reading an unfamiliar codebase and making a small change to it; you do not
    need distributed systems theory, and the course does not assume you have
    run anything in production.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will spend most of the semester making failures happen on purpose,
    then deciding what to do about them. You will leave with an audit, a retry
    policy you have defended out loud against someone briefed to break it, and
    a written argument for not retrying at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve situations, in order, and what each one costs.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>An audit, a policy you defend, and a case you argue. What each is worth, and when.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course, what to bring them, and when they are free.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .thesis {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin-block: 0 2.5rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1rem;
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--at-accent);
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
